I'd put the account check into each `WHERE` clause, using `req.user.accountId` (which `auth.js:11` already sets), and return 404 when no row comes back.

**`routes/orders.js`, cancel:**

```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

**`routes/accounts.js`:** a user belongs to exactly one account, so `:id` is only valid when it matches that account. Add the same condition to both statements:

```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});

router.delete("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = $1 AND id = $2 RETURNING id",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.status(204).end();
});
```

The 404 check matters because `db.write` returns `undefined` when nothing matches. Right now a cross-account request gets a blank 200 or a 204, so the caller can't tell anything failed. Use 404 rather than 403 so other accounts' ids aren't confirmed to exist.

There's one real choice to make. Because `db.write` is the only path for writes, it could run `SET LOCAL app.account_id` inside its transaction and let Postgres row-level security enforce scoping for every future route too. The cost is that both `write()` and `query()` would need to take the account id. `query()` uses `pool.query` without a transaction, so reads wouldn't be covered until it's changed. I'd ship the `WHERE` clauses now and treat row-level security as a later defence-in-depth step.

Separately, this fixes cross-account access only. Any user can still rename or soft-delete their own account. Nothing in `users` that `auth.js` selects describes a role, so if deleting the account should be admin-only, that needs a role check too.

