```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();
  res.on("close", () => {
    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      method: req.method,
      path: req.originalUrl,
      status: res.statusCode,
      ms: Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6,
      userId: req.user && req.user.id,
      accountId: req.user && req.user.accountId,
      completed: res.writableFinished,
    }));
  });
  next();
});
```

`req.user` is set by the time `close` fires, so each line is attributed to the account without touching the routes. Use `close` rather than `finish` so that aborted requests are logged too (`completed: false`). A full process crash still won't be logged.

Don't log cookies or request bodies. The `session` cookie is a bearer credential, so anyone who reads the logs could take over that session. The account `PATCH` body also carries `billing_email`.

What's left to decide is where the lines go. If you already have a log pipeline, `pino-http` set up with the same fields and redaction fits it better than `console.log`. Which one do you run?

